"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016119"} "Kayak Singles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016138"} "Kayak Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016157"} "Kayak Fours, 500 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016188"} "Canadian Doubles, 1,000 metres, Men"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016207"} "Kayak Singles, 200 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016226"} "Kayak Singles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016245"} "Kayak Doubles, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016264"} "Kayak Fours, 500 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016276"} "Canadian Singles, 200 metres, Women"] "\n" [:option {:value "19016295"} "Canadian Doubles, 500 metres, Women"]] [:script {} "$('#same_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#same_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:form {:class "form-inline form-inline-header"} [:select {:name "id", :id "other_games_select", :class "form-control"} [:option {:value ""} "In other Games"] "\n" [:option {:value "255015"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "255039"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "255067"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "255104"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "255191"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "255264"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "255328"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "255393"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "255521"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "255645"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "255751"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "255861"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "256002"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "256132"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "256253"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "256367"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "258793"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "307026"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "353521"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "2008542"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/169"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Canadian Singles, 1,000 metres, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "6 –  7 August 2021"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/2129"} "Sea Forest Waterway, 6-44, 3chome, Uminomori, Koto-ku, Tokyo Bay, Tokyo, Japan"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/19016169"} "32 from 19 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was the 20th appearance of the event, which was one of four to be held at every Summer Games since canoeing was introduced in 1936."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The reigning World Champion was " [:a {:href "/athletes/132801"} "Isaquias Queiroz"] ", who also took the silver medal at Rio in 2016. One of the other contenders was " [:a {:href "/athletes/125320"} "Sebastian Brendel"] " of Germany, the two-time Olympic and four-time world champion in this distance. He had not been in his best form prior to the Games but was experienced enough to time his Olympic campaign to perfection. Another favourite was " [:a {:href "/athletes/133417"} "Martin Fuksa"] " of Czech Republic, a World Championship silver medallist behind Brendel. The second Czech competitor was his younger brother, " [:a {:href "/athletes/142093"} "Petr"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "On the first day in the preliminary heats, both Queiroz and Martin Fuksa won their races and qualified directly into the semi-finals. Brendel finished only third and had to start again in the quarter-finals. In the semi-finals Queiroz and " [:a {:href "/athletes/133829"} "Adrien Bart"] " of France, the bronze medallist from the 2019 World Championship, won their races. Brendel finished seventh and could not qualify for the A Final. In the A Final, Queiroz and " [:a {:href "/athletes/141833"} "Liu Hao"] " of China were pacing each other in the first quarter of the race, but the Brazilian broke away in the last 250 metres and finished first. Liu came in second behind Queiroz, while " [:a {:href "/athletes/135100"} "Serghei Tarnovschi"] " from the Republic of Moldova came from behind and overtook Bart in the final second to finish third. Five years before, Tarnovschi finished third as well, but due to a positive doping test, his result was deleted and he was sentenced to a four-year ban in 2017. \nAfter winning two silvers and a bronze in Rio, Queiroz finally won the first gold medal for Brazil. He dedicated his victory to his former coach, who died of a brain tumour in 2018."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "QF"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "38"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/132801"} "Isaquias Queiroz"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/BRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/BRA.png"}] "BRA"]] [:td {} "3:59.894 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "4:05.579 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "4:04.408 (1 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "58"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/141833"} "Liu Hao"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CHN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CHN.png"}] "CHN"]] [:td {} "4:06.914 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "4:04.196 (2 h1)"] [:td {} "4:05.724 (2 h1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "168"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/135100"} "Serghei Tarnovschi"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/MDA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/MDA.png"}] "MDA"]] [:td {} "4:02.794 (1 h4)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "4:06